1. What is CSS?
Answer: CSS is used to make a web page look nice. It changes things like colors, text, and layout.
Example:
  p {
    color: blue;
  }
This makes paragraph text blue.

2. What are the types of CSS?
Answer: There are three ways to add CSS:
- Inline: written inside an HTML tag.
  <p style="color: blue;">Hello</p>
- Internal: written inside a <style> tag in the HTML file.
  <style>
    p { color: blue; }
  </style>
- External: written in a separate .css file.
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">

3. Which type of CSS is best and why?
Answer: External CSS is usually best. It keeps styles in one separate file, so they are easier to change and reuse on many pages.
Example:
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">

4. What is the CSS box model?
Answer: A web page element is like a box. It has content in the middle, padding around the content, a border around the padding, and margin outside the border.
Example:
  .box {
    padding: 10px;
    border: 2px solid black;
    margin: 8px;
  }

5. What is CSS Grid?
Answer: CSS Grid helps place items in rows and columns, like items on a shelf.
Example:
  .grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 10px;
  }
This makes two columns.

6. What is responsive web design?
Answer: Responsive design makes a web page fit different screen sizes, such as phones, tablets, and computers.
Example:
  img {
    max-width: 100%;
  }
This keeps an image from being wider than its container.

7. What is CSS specificity?
Answer: Specificity is how the browser decides which style to use when more than one style matches. A style for an ID usually beats a style for a class, and a class usually beats a tag name.
Example:
  p { color: black; }
  .note { color: blue; }
  #warning { color: red; }
If an element has id="warning", its text will be red.

8. What is margin collapsing and when does it occur?
Answer: Sometimes the vertical margins of two nearby blocks combine into one space instead of being added together.
Example:
  .first { margin-bottom: 20px; }
  .second { margin-top: 30px; }
The space between these blocks is usually 30px, not 50px.

9. What is the difference between Flexbox and Grid?
Answer: Flexbox is best for lining items up in one direction, like a row or a column. Grid is best for laying items out in rows and columns.
Example:
  .menu { display: flex; }
  .photo-gallery { display: grid; }

10. What is Flexbox?
Answer: Flexbox helps line up items in a row or a column and control the space between them.
Example:
  .menu {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10px;
  }
This puts menu items in a row with space between them.

11. What is CSS Grid?
Answer: CSS Grid is a way to arrange items in rows and columns. For example, it can make a gallery with three columns.
Example:
  .gallery {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 10px;
  }

12. What are media queries?
Answer: Media queries let you use different styles for different screen sizes.
Example:
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .menu {
      display: block;
    }
  }
On screens 600px wide or smaller, the menu items are placed one under another.

13. What are CSS animations?
Answer: Animations change an element's style over time, such as making it slowly appear.
Example:
  @keyframes appear {
    from { opacity: 0; }
    to { opacity: 1; }
  }
  .message {
    animation: appear 1s;
  }

14. What is the universal selector?
Answer: The universal selector (*) selects every element on the page.
Example:
  * {
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }
This applies the box-sizing rule to every element.

15. What are CSS transitions?
Answer: A transition makes a style change happen smoothly, for example when you move your mouse over a button.
Example:
  button {
    background-color: blue;
    transition: 0.3s;
  }
  button:hover {
    background-color: green;
  }
